title: Urticaria, Wheals and Angioedema
---

# Urticaria, Wheals and Angioedema

- **Definition and terminology:**
  - <u>Urticaria</u> - a condition characterised by the development of wheals (hives), angioedema, or both
  - <u>Wheals</u> - skin lesion of a fleeting nature (\< 24h), a/w central swelling and pruritis
  - <u>Angioedema</u> - swelling of the deep dermis or subcutaneous tissue, painful rather than pruritic, with slower resolution ~ up to 72h

  
  ![](../img/relationship_between_urticaria_angioedema_and_wheals.png)
- **Two endotypes of angioedema** - different underlying pathomechanisms, with overlapping but distinct clinical features:
  - **Histaminergic angioedema** - caused by <u>mast cell degranulation</u> in an allergic, or non-allergic manner:
    - <u>Allergic vs non-allergic mast-cell degranulation</u>:
      - **Allergic reaction** - caused by binding of allergen (e.g. food, drugs) to IgE on mast cells to induce mast cell degranulation
      - **Non-allergic reaction** - mast cell degranulation by chemicals, drugs or other triggers
    - <u>Effects of products of mast-cell degranulation</u> - dependent on site of degranulation:
      - **Stimulation of sensory nerve** - manifesting as **pruritis**, occular itching or sneezing
      - **Vasodilation and increased vascular permeability** - manifesting as **wheels** / angioedema in the skin, rhinorhea and sputum in the airways
      - **Contraction of smooth muscles** - manifesting as wheezing due to bronchoconstriction or diarrhoea and abdominal pain due to increased peristalsis

      
      ![](../img/effects_of_mast_cell_degranulation.png)
  - **Bradykinergic angioedema** - bradykinin generated from the Kallikrein-Bradykinin pathway:
    - <u>Kallikrein-Bradykinin pathway</u> - plasma contact pathway regulated by **C1-esterase inhibitor**:
      - C1 esterase inhibitor inhibits conversion of prekallikrein to kallikrein
      - Kallikren responsible for conversion of HMWK to bradykinin
      - ACE is responsible for inhibition of bradykinin production

      
      ![](../img/kallikrein_bradykinin_pathway.png)
    - <u>Effects of bradykinin</u>:
      - **Pain stimulus on sensory nerve** - results in pain
      - **Vasodilation and increased vascular permeability** - causes angioedema
- **Clinical classification of angioedema:**
  - <u>Angioedema with wheals</u> (i.e. pruritis) - histaminergic angioedema
  - <u>Angioedema w/o wheals</u> (i.e. absence of pruritis) - histaminergic or bradykinergic angioedema

  
  ![](../img/clinical_differentiation_between_histaminergic_or_bradykinergic_angioedema.png)
- **DDx of angioedema** - classified by pathomechanism:
  - **Histaminergic angioedema** - presentation w/ or w/o wheals:
    - Allergic reaction (including anaphylaxis)
    - Chronic spontaneous urticaria
    - Inducible urticaria and angioedema
    - Urticarial vasculitis
    - Auto-inflammatory syndromes
  - **Bradykinergic angioedema** - presentation w/o wheals:
    - ACEi-associated angioedema
    - Hereditary angioedema
    - Acquired C1-esterase inhibitor deficiency

  <!-- -->

  - **Salient points of Hx:**
    - **HPI** - site, onset, duration, quality, and associated exposures:
      - <u>Site</u> - characterise site of eruption, as bradykinergic angioedema has a tendancy of developing over the face, upper extremities or around genitalia
      - <u>Onset</u> - urticaria present for \< 6 weeks is considered to be acute, and chronic if \> 6 weeks (CSU is the most common cause of chronic urticaria)
      - <u>Duration of eruption</u>:
        - Individual wheals typically resolve within 24h
        - Angioedema typically takes longer to resolver (24-72h)
      - <u>Quality</u>:
        - Wheals are typically pruritic w/ no pain
        - Angioedema are typically painful, and not pruritic
      - <u>Associated exposures</u> - note exposure within the last 24h:
        - Food
        - Medications
        - Inhalants
        - Cotact (e.g. latex)
    - **Associated Sx:**
      - <u>Swelling of the face</u> - note any swelling of the lips, face, tongue
      - <u>Wheezing or SOB</u> - suggest respiratory comprimise
      - <u>Abdominal pain</u> - suggest GI comprimise
    - **Drug Hx**
    - **FHx** - FHx of angioedema
  - **P/E:**
    - <u>Active wheals</u>: 
    ![](../img/wheals.png)
  - **Ix:**
    - **Routine bloods** - CBC, LRFT, ESR, TFT, iron studies
    - **Total IgE and specific IgE** - to possible allergens e.g. shellfish, peanutes, house dust mites
    - **ANA** - +ve for SLE and urticarial vasculitis
    - **C3/4** - consider C1 esterase inhibitor activity if low
    - +/- **skin Bx** - if urticarial vasculitis suspected
    - **+/- challenge test** - to confirm physical urticaria
  - **Mx:**
    - **Principles of Mx:**
      - <u>Removal of trigger</u> - detailed allergic Hx to identify inciting allergen
      - <u>Pharmacological therapy</u> - non-sedating antihistamines are first line, with mast cell stabilisers, leuktriene receptor antagonists, or even systemic therapy
